  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn elevated heat risk will be the name of the game over the \nnext few days low level flow generally SW to W over the next \nseveral days. As a result, some weak WAA will allow low level \nthicknesses to increase closer to 1430m. Based off the offices \nclimatological study this is forecast to result in highs in the \nlow to mid 90s across the inland areas and upper 80s to low 90s \nalong the OBX and coast through much of the week before a front \nimpacts the region resulting in cooler temperatures late week \ninto the weekend. \n\nHeat indices are expected to peak around 100-110 each afternoon\nthrough Thurs, and potentially lingering into Fri with daily heat\nheadlines probable for much of the area. For today, have \nexpanded the Heat Advisory to include Pitt, Greene, and Lenoir\ncounties. We encourage anyone planning on spending alot of time\noutdoors this week to take proper precautions and stay \nhydrated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA typical summer time pattern will remain in place through much\nthe week with a series of shortwaves/MCS's moving through WNW \nflow aloft bringing periods of showers and thunderstorms across\nthe region. With a moist and unstable airmass in place, could \nsee daily seabreeze related storms developing each afternoon, \nhowever best forcing will occur as individual shortwaves move \nacross the region. \n\nA MCS currently over WV/Western VA is expected to dive into NC\nlate afternoon, reaching the eastern half of NC this evening.\nThere is a lot of uncertainty on the coverage and strength of\nstorms as it approaches eastern NC, but if it can keep its\nstrength, some gusty winds could be at play with the strongest\nof tstorms this evening into early tonight. \n\nA second MCS currently moving through Indiana is expected to\ndive southeast and reach NC later tonight. How the first MCS\nprogresses has a big impact on the second MCS, so there is once\nagain low confidence on the outcome. Regardless, gusty winds are\nstill at play with and stronger storms that exist as the second\nMCS remnant approaches eastern NC.\n\nSPC has the entire CWA in a marginal risk for severe weather\ntonight.\n\nAdditional shortwaves are expected tomorrow and Thursday PM and\nSPC has portions of the FA in a marginal risk of severe \nthunderstorms for Day 2 as these systems push across the area. \nDamaging wind gusts will be the primary severe threat with these\nstorms. A stronger northern stream shortwave is progged to push\nacross the Mid- Atlantic Friday with the attendant cold front \npushing into the area bringing the best forcing and chances for \nwidespread showers and storms across the area. High pressure \nbuild into the area over the weekend bringing bringing a drier \nairmass and minimal chances for storms.",
